digraph ReasoningTree {
  rankdir="TB";
  "NROOT" [label="(0,0,0) Develop and evaluate controlled external electrical stimulation during ex vivo CD8+ T-cell and CAR-T preparation to test whether it enhances adoptive cell therapy by modulating TCR signaling, EGR1-associated chromatin programming, proliferation, and effector differentiation.", fillcolor="lightyellow", style="filled"];
  "NROOT_COMMON" [label="(0,0,0) When ACT efficacy is limited by CD8+ T-cell quality, ex vivo culture is a manipulable preparation window, and TCR/EGR1-linked TF/chromatin programs are responsive to biophysical or electrical cues, it is scientifically justified to develop and evaluate culture interventions that tune those programs."];
  "R10" [label="(0,0,0) EGR1-centered effects on CD8+ T-cell responses should be interpreted in the context of antigen recognition through the TCR-CD3 signaling pathway."];
  "R13" [label="(0,0,0) Some biophysical culture properties, such as substrate rigidity, should be considered controllable parameters when developing T-cell culture systems because they can influence activation, proliferation, and differentiation."];
  "R14" [label="(0,0,0) Given that some biophysical culture properties can influence T-cell activation states and electrical/biophysical cues could affect receptor organization and signaling, external electrical stimulation during ex vivo culture is a rational intervention to investigate for modulating TCR signaling and downstream TF/chromatin states."];
  "R18" [label="(0,0,0) The brief in vitro activation and expansion step before reinfusion is a clinically relevant window in which ex vivo preparation improvements could be pursued."];
  "R19" [label="(0,0,0) EGR1's known importance in T-cell processes, including virus-specific CD8+ T-cell clonal expansion, supports investigating antigen-dependent EGR1 modulation during ex vivo culture as a possible way to influence proliferative potential and functional fate."];
  "R2" [label="(0,0,0) Because ex vivo preparation influences CD8+ product quality and culture optimization/mechanistic elucidation are already identified as critical, optimizing ex vivo culture conditions is a plausible priority for improving ACT utility."];
  "R21" [label="(0,0,0) Because physical characteristics can affect the tumor microenvironment and CAR-T efficacy, and biophysical cues are increasingly recognized as important in CD8+ T-cell activation, physical and biophysical properties are relevant considerations for optimizing therapeutic T-cell function."];
  "R22" [label="(0,0,0) Because prolonged ex vivo culturing depletes CAR T-cell stemness and ex vivo preparation influences CD8+ product quality, culture interventions should be designed to avoid unnecessary loss of high-quality stem-like or proliferative states."];
  "R23" [label="(0,0,0) An ex vivo ACT-improvement strategy should include culture optimization and identification of modulators of TF/chromatin programming during CD8+ T-cell preparation."];
  "R24" [label="(0,0,0) Electrical or charge-based culture interventions that affect EGR1 expression and EGR1-site chromatin accessibility are plausible interventions to test for enhancing CD8+ proliferative potential and polarization control."];
  "R25" [label="(0,0,0) External electrical stimulation should be tested during ex vivo CD8+ T-cell culture in a defined antigen/TCR stimulation context to evaluate whether it modulates TCR signaling and downstream TF/chromatin trajectories."];
  "R26" [label="(0,0,0) The paper should investigate controlled external electrical stimulation during ex vivo CD8+ T-cell preparation as a strategy to modulate TCR-linked TF/chromatin programming, including EGR1-associated programs."];
  "R27" [label="(0,0,0) Because ACT efficacy in solid tumors is compromised by impaired CD8+ T-cell function, activation, and lifespan, interventions that improve ex vivo CD8+ activation, proliferation, and fate are needed to improve ACT outcomes."];
  "R3" [label="(0,0,0) Because EGR1 is rapidly induced by TCR stimulation and links early TCR signaling to downstream gene expression, regulators of the TCR-to-TF/chromatin axis are plausible targets for reprogramming ex vivo CD8+ T-cell products."];
  "R7" [label="(0,0,0) EGR1 is a plausible, testable mechanistic lever for improving ex vivo CD8+ T-cell products because it links early TCR signaling to gene expression and increasing antigen-dependent EGR1 activity may enhance proliferative potential and functional fate."];
  "n_a1" [label="(0,0,0) abduction-reasoning: Given it is elusive how electrical signals convert to biochemical signaling in T cells and given electrical properties affect receptor mobility/electrostatics/synapse oscillations, therefore a plausible hypothesis is that manipulating extracellular electrical cues could alter TCR signaling in T cells."];
  "n_a2" [label="(0,0,0) abduction-reasoning: Given charged substrate increases EGR1 expression and EGR1-site chromatin accessibility and given EGR1 is pivotal for CD8+ responses, therefore a plausible explanation is that electrical/charge cues tune key TF/chromatin programs controlling CD8+ proliferation/polarization."];
  "n_b1" [label="(0,0,0) deduction-reasoning: Given ACT is a promising oncology therapy and if ACT efficacy depends on robust CD8+ activation/proliferation/fate, therefore improving CD8+ product quality can increase the realized benefit of ACT."];
  "n_b3" [label="(0,0,0) deduction-reasoning: Given ACT relies on ex vivo activation/expansion before infusion and if ex vivo preparation influences outcomes, therefore ex vivo culture is a direct intervention point for ACT improvement."];
  "n_b6" [label="(0,0,0) deduction-reasoning: Given electrical stimulation is feasible in other biomedical contexts (e.g., piezoelectric membranes) and if electrical cues can modulate receptor signaling programs, therefore implementing controlled electrical stimulation in ex vivo immune-cell culture is technically plausible."];
  "n_d2" [label="(0,0,0) deduction-reasoning: Given the ex vivo activation/expansion phase influences efficacy and if improving ex vivo preparation can improve outcomes, therefore optimizing the ex vivo culture phase is a key handle to improve ACT."];
  "n_d5" [label="(0,0,0) deduction-reasoning: Given TCR engagement is reinforced by clustering and if clustering leads to an immune synapse that activates TFs including EGR1, therefore TCR clustering/synapse formation triggers TF programs including EGR1."];
  "n_d6" [label="(0,0,0) deduction-reasoning: Given TFs regulate chromatin accessibility shaping fate and if TF/chromatin programs determine CD8+ fate, therefore modulating TF-driven chromatin accessibility is a mechanism to control CD8+ fate."];
  "n_d7" [label="(0,0,0) deduction-reasoning: Given identifying regulators of TF activity/specification is crucial and if modulating TCR→TF→chromatin can reprogram products, therefore we should identify modulators of TF activity (including EGR1-centered modulators)."];
  "n_f4" [label="(0,0,0) abduction-reasoning: Given improvement efforts typically focus on biological/chemical interventions and given biophysical cues are increasingly recognized as critical in CD8+ activation, therefore a plausible research direction is to develop biophysical/electrical modulation during ex vivo culture to improve T-cell products."];
  "n_i1" [label="(0,0,0) If ACT efficacy depends on robust CD8+ T-cell activation/proliferation/fate, then interventions that improve ex vivo CD8+ T-cell activation/proliferation/fate should improve ACT outcomes."];
  "n_i2" [label="(0,0,0) If ACT outcomes in solid tumors are limited and ex vivo preparation influences CD8+ product quality, then improving the ex vivo preparation phase is a plausible route to improve outcomes."];
  "n_i3" [label="(0,0,0) If TCR signaling and downstream TF/chromatin programs determine CD8+ T-cell fate, then modulating regulators of TCR→TF→chromatin can reprogram ex vivo T-cell products."];
  "n_i4" [label="(0,0,0) If EGR1 is a key TF linking early TCR signaling to gene expression and proliferation, then increasing antigen-dependent EGR1 activity during ex vivo culture may enhance proliferative potential and functional fate of CD8+ T cells."];
  "n_i5" [label="(0,0,0) If electrical/biophysical cues affect receptor organization/mobility and signaling, then external electrical stimulation during ex vivo culture could modulate TCR signaling and downstream TF/chromatin states."];
  "n_r31_27" [label="(21,31,27) Currently, substrate rigidity should be a consideration in developing T-cell culture systems because it influences T-cell activation/proliferation/differentiation."];
  "n_r9_7" [label="(6,9,7) Currently, prolonged ex vivo culturing depletes CAR T-cell stemness (Tstem)."];
  "n_s1" [label="(1,0,0) Currently, ACT is a promising oncology therapy that can precisely target and eliminate cancer cells."];
  "n_s10" [label="(10,0,0) Currently, TCR clustering leads to immune synapse formation that activates TFs (including EGR1/NFAT/AP-1) driving effector-gene transcription."];
  "n_s11" [label="(11,0,0) Currently, these TFs regulate chromatin accessibility, shaping T-cell function and fate determination."];
  "n_s12" [label="(12,0,0) Currently, identifying molecular regulators that modulate TF activity and guide T-cell specification is crucial for enhancing antitumor responses."];
  "n_s13" [label="(13,0,0) Currently, EGR1 is a pivotal transcription factor in CD8+ T-cell responses."];
  "n_s14" [label="(14,0,0) Currently, EGR1 is rapidly induced upon TCR stimulation and links early TCR signaling to downstream gene expression."];
  "n_s15" [label="(15,0,0) Currently, EGR1 is important for T-cell processes (cytokines, development) and is crucial for clonal expansion of virus-specific CD8+ T cells."];
  "n_s16" [label="(16,0,0) Currently, electrical stimulation via piezoelectric membranes has been demonstrated to accelerate bone regeneration/combats bacteria (showing feasibility of bioelectric modulation)."];
  "n_s17" [label="(17,0,0) Currently, treating CD8+ T cells with a charged substrate enhances EGR1 expression and increases chromatin accessibility at EGR1 binding sites, driving proliferation/polarization."];
  "n_s18" [label="(18,0,0) Currently, EGR1 activation is antigen-dependent and shapes CD8+ T-cell responses across immune contexts."];
  "n_s19" [label="(19,0,0) Currently, clinically, autologous CD8+ T cells are genetically modified then briefly activated/expanded in vitro before reinfusion."];
  "n_s2" [label="(2,0,0) Currently, in solid tumors ACT efficacy is often compromised due to impaired CD8+ T-cell function, inadequate activation, and limited lifespan of tumor-reactive T cells."];
  "n_s20" [label="(20,0,0) Currently, attempts to improve T-cell functionality typically focus on biological/chemical interventions."];
  "n_s21" [label="(21,0,0) Currently, although biochemical factors are well-studied, biophysical cues (shear/solid stress/matrix stiffness) are increasingly recognized as critical in CD8+ T-cell activation."];
  "n_s22" [label="(22,0,0) Currently, physical characteristics can independently or synergistically impact tumor microenvironment and CAR-T efficacy."];
  "n_s23" [label="(23,0,0) Currently, membrane potential depolarization affects receptor mobility; synapse oscillations and electrostatic interactions in TCR signaling regulation are observed."];
  "n_s24" [label="(24,0,0) Currently, it remains elusive how electrical signals convert to biochemical signaling in T cells and whether external electrical stimulation can modulate T-cell activity in cancer therapy."];
  "n_s3" [label="(3,0,0) Currently, tumor antigen recognition through the TCR-CD3 complex initiates downstream signaling essential for robust immune responses."];
  "n_s5" [label="(5,0,0) Currently, ACT relies on ex vivo activation and expansion of T cells (including CAR-T) before infusion."];
  "n_s6" [label="(6,0,0) Currently, evidence indicates the ex vivo activation/expansion phase can influence the therapeutic efficacy of tumor-specific CD8+ T cells."];
  "n_s7" [label="(7,0,0) Currently, optimizing culture conditions and elucidating mechanisms are critical to broaden ACT clinical utility."];
  "n_s9" [label="(9,0,0) Currently, after TCR engagement, antigen recognition is reinforced by formation of TCR clusters at the membrane."];
  "n_s6" -> "n_d2" [label="deduction-case"];
  "n_i2" -> "n_d2" [label="deduction-rule"];
  "n_s9" -> "n_d5" [label="deduction-case"];
